            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new run of words: what did the last two words of the chapter before mean?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: चावल",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "चावल (chāval) — \"rice\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Sanskrit's own word for rice is तण्डुल (taṇḍula), and it is not the ancestor of this one. Where chāval came from is genuinely unsettled — the dictionaries record the Prakrit forms behind it without agreeing on what stands behind those."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That makes it the fourth everyday Hindi word you have met whose origin is honestly unknown, after बिल्ली, कुत्ता and पेट. It is worth noticing the pattern: the words a language uses most are not always the words whose history it kept."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The first of five things a kitchen is stocked with."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
