        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: चीनी",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "चीनी (chīnī) — \"sugar\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "चीनी (chīnī) means, quite literally, 'Chinese'. The everyday Hindi word for sugar names the country the refining technique was associated with — the same shape as calling a thing china because of where it was made."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The traffic ran the other way too. English sugar came through Arabic sukkar from Sanskrit शर्करा (śarkarā), 'grit, gravel' — so India exported the word for sugar to Europe and then named its own everyday sugar after China. Both halves of that are true at once."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The fourth of five, and the only one that is a nationality."
            }
          ]
        },
